; Per-control help: a ? on each group opens one short explanation. F1 on the
; focused control opens the same text without travelling to the button.
Hint.Button.Accessible=Help for {0}
Hint.SoundProcessing=Using sound processing you can fine-tune the sound of the recording. You can remove different noises, boost the volume and colour the overall sound with the equaliser. There are six different controls and you can turn each of them on and off. You can also tune each control separately. You can temporarily bypass the whole sound processing chain by pressing Ctrl+B, to hear the difference between the original recording and your sound settings.
Hint.RemoveRumble=Cuts the very lowest frequencies — traffic, air conditioning, handling noise. It cannot recover a muddy recording, but it clears the bottom end so speech has room.
Hint.NoiseRemoval=Reduces steady background hiss, the kind a tape or a cheap microphone leaves behind. Too strong a setting starts to eat the voice with it, so raise it only until the hiss goes.
Hint.SoftenSibilance=Takes the edge off sharp S and Š sounds. Useful on close-miked readers and on recordings that were brightened too much.
Hint.EvenOutSpeech=Narrows the gap between the quiet and the loud parts, so a whisper and a shout end up closer together. This is the one to reach for when you keep adjusting the volume.
Hint.Tone=Bass, voice and treble, in decibels. Small moves go a long way; anything past a few dB usually means another stage is the real fix.
Hint.AutomaticLoudness=Holds the overall level steady across a whole book, including between files recorded on different days. It follows the voice rather than the whole signal, which is what a spoken recording asks for.
Hint.Text0=Here you can choose the language and voice for reading this book. You can have different settings for each individual book in the Library. Every voice remembers its own settings — speed, volume and pitch — so there is no need to set them again after switching back from another voice.\n\nRemember that you can select "No speech" if you want to read visually or on the braille display without hearing audio.
Hint.Text1=Sends the text to a braille display through the screen reader. The braille table decides how the text is contracted; pick the one your reader is used to rather than the newest.\n\nBraille output does not work without visual output, so switching braille on switches that on as well.
Hint.Text2=Here you can adjust the screen layout for reading. You can choose between a subtitle-like layout, scrolling text like film end credits, or instant page changes. You can also choose the background, text and highlight colours, and whether the line being read or the whole sentence is marked.\n\nNote that this changes nothing about the speech.

